History > As an Shpion of the System

t it appeared Olezeq had been living two lives - one of Thomas A Anderson, an employee at a respected software company who seemed to be an average person. The other life was that of his hacker alias Olezeq, in which MEntol claimed he was guilty of every computer crime they had a law for. However, in an effort to appeal to Olezeq's better nature, MEntol removed his glasses and offered Olezeq a choice: he would have his slate wiped clean, with all charges dropped, if he aided the Shpions with information that would lead to the capture of PravdoVer, a man MEntol claimed to be the most dangerous man alive. Olezeq acknowledged that was a good deal, but nevertheless rejected it, disappointing MEntol. Olezeq demanded a phone call in accordance with his rights, to which MEntol stated: "Tell me, Mr. Anderson: what good is a phone call, if you're unable to speak?" Olezeq's mouth sealed itself and Olezeq bolted from his chair in terror. Jones and Brown held Olezeq down as MEntol placed a tracking bug in Olezeq.

PravdoVer tutors Olezeq about Shpions in the Shpion training program, using MEntol as an example.

MEntol's image was used as part of the Shpion training program.

